            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary consensus interprets the ship as a symbol of the Church (the vessel of salvation carrying believers safely through the storms of the world). A widely recognized alternative meaning in prophetic literature connects ships to worldly commerce, human pride, and economic kingdoms like Tyre and Babylon.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book identifies the ship as an earthly kingdom, city, state, or governed political body. It actively rejects the interpretation of the ship as the Church, stating that Christ needs no such 'constructed body' beneath Him, though He may become its Head.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The Church (primary) or worldly commercial powers/kingdoms (secondary).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"An earthly kingdom, city, state, or governed political body.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus restricts the 'worldly power' interpretation to specific prophetic passages (e.g., Ezekiel 27, Revelation 18) while treating Gospel boats and Noah's Ark as the Church. The book applies the 'earthly kingdom' interpretation universally, reclassifying the Gospel boats as earthly institutions rather than the Church.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus equates the ship primarily with the Church (the spiritual body of Christ and vessel of salvation); the book explicitly denies this ('Christ needs no constructed body beneath Him') and identifies the ship solely as a human, earthly political institution.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book elevates a secondary consensus alternative (worldly kingdoms/commerce) to the universal meaning, directly contradicting the primary consensus by rejecting the longstanding ecclesial identification of the ship.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Applying the 'earthly kingdom' definition to the Gospel narratives of Jesus and the disciples in boats ignores the profound and early ecclesial typology rooted in Noah's Ark and Christ's stilling of the storm, which the Church has overwhelmingly understood as depicting its own salvation and mission.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
